## Reference Depth Standard

Use the same style as a production platform reference:

- name the exact directive, API field, GitOps file, or environment variable
- state whether the field is required, optional, defaulted, inherited, or
  mutually exclusive
- list supported option values when the code constrains them
- explain runtime behavior and authorization behavior separately
- include a minimal YAML or command example for complex feature areas
- summarize long operational workflows directly in the wiki and keep focused
  repo docs as verification evidence only

Avoid marketing summaries in reference articles. The user should be able to
answer practical questions such as:

- "Can a task set `agent_profile`?" No. Tasks can set `llm_profile`, but
  `agent_profile` is valid only at pipeline and step level.
- "Does `steps[].llm_profile` grant access to a provider?" No. It selects the
  named LLM profile; AAA and profile scope restrictions still decide whether the
  original caller may use it.
- "Can a script task use `mcp_profiles`?" No. Explicit MCP profiles are valid
  only for LLM-backed goal work.
- "What happens when `llm_enabled: false`?" The pipeline must be script-only and
  cannot define goals, step conditions, final outputs, or explicit MCP profile
  use.
- "Can a dashboard output define `dashboard.*` fields when type is `pdf`?" No.
  Dashboard target fields require `type: dashboard`.

## Pipeline YAML Directive Reference

The in-app article **Pipeline YAML Schema** owns top-level pipeline fields.
It should include at least these directives:

| Directive | Scope | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `name` | pipeline | Required stable name. Letters, numbers, underscores, dots, and hyphens are supported. |
| `version` | pipeline | Optional version. Runtime validation defaults it to `latest` when omitted. |
| `description` | pipeline | Human-readable description for UI/API review context. |
| `container_image` | pipeline | Default executable image. Required unless every executable step has its own image; approval-only steps do not need one. |
| `working_directory` | pipeline | Container working directory. Empty or `.` resolves to `/workspace`; relative paths are rooted under `/workspace`; root, colon, NUL, and escaping paths are rejected. |
| `display_options.github_view` | pipeline | GitHub check visualization hint such as `mermaid`, `tree`, or `flat`. |
| `variables` | pipeline | Runtime variable references required by the pipeline. Bare names resolve in the run scope; `default:NAME` resolves unscoped/default; `scope/path:NAME` resolves an explicit scope and injects `NAME`. |
| `steps` | pipeline | Required non-empty ordered list of step definitions. |
| `timeout` | pipeline | Optional whole-run duration such as `45m` or `2h`. |
| `llm_enabled` | pipeline | Optional boolean. `false` is for script-only pipelines and rejects goals, conditions, final outputs, and MCP profile validation. |
| `agent_profile` | pipeline | Default AI persona and instruction profile. Step can override it. |
| `llm_profile` | pipeline | Default model-provider profile. Step, task, output, and output item can override it where supported. |
| `mcp_profiles` | pipeline | Approved MCP tool profiles available to LLM goal work. Additive with step and task MCP profiles. |
| `runtime_pool` | pipeline | Kubernetes runtime pool default. Docker runners ignore it. |
| `affinity_enabled` | pipeline | Kubernetes same-node affinity override for the run. |
| `knowledge_context` | pipeline | Knowledge references merged into all effective LLM task contexts. |
| `llm_content_sharing` | pipeline | Optional boolean. Defaults to `false`; set `true` only when workspace file listings/content should be shared automatically with LLM goal resolution. Bounded workspace tools can still retrieve current file identities and file content on demand. Shared and retrieved files include path, SHA-256, size, and workspace revision metadata so stale file replacements can be rejected. |
| `llm_content_include` | pipeline | Include filters for files shared with LLM goal context. |
| `llm_content_ignore` | pipeline | Ignore filters for files shared with LLM goal context. |
| `llm_output_sharing` | pipeline | Default for whether task output is written into later LLM execution history. |
| `output` | pipeline | Final deliverables and dashboard publications generated after run execution. |

The article **Step and Task Directives** owns step and task fields. Every step
must define exactly one execution mode:

| Mode | Field | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Include | `include` | `step:<identifier>` includes a reusable step. `pipeline:<identifier>` launches a child pipeline. |
| Multi-task | `tasks` | Each task defines exactly one of `goal` or `script`; task dependencies are local to the same step. |
| Single LLM goal | `goal` | LLM-backed single-task step. Requires LLM to be enabled. |
| Single script | `script` | Direct shell step. With guardrail/policy Knowledge Context, the exact command is LLM-validated before execution. |
| Approval | `approval` | Durable human checkpoint that pauses the run and releases runner capacity. |

Common step directives:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `steps[].name` | Required unique step name. Step dependencies reference this name. |
| `steps[].image` | Step executable image override. |
| `steps[].depends_on` | Step dependencies. Values must be other step names. |
| `steps[].condition` | LLM-evaluated natural-language condition. False normally skips; false under blocking guardrail/policy context fails closed. |
| `steps[].secrets` | Scoped secret refs injected as environment variables. |
| `steps[].variables` | Step-wide environment overrides. |
| `steps[].volumes` | Docker volume mounts using `volume:/mount/path`. |
| `steps[].ignore_failure` | Treats failure as ignored so graph progress can continue. |
| `steps[].llm_output_sharing` | Step-level output-history sharing override. |
| `steps[].agent_profile` | Step persona override. Valid for conditions and LLM goals in the step. |
| `steps[].llm_profile` | Step model-client override. Applies to the step condition and tasks unless a task has `llm_profile`. |
| `steps[].mcp_profiles` | MCP profiles added for LLM goals in the step. Invalid on script-only and include steps. |
| `steps[].runtime_pool` | Kubernetes runtime pool override. Falls back to pipeline `runtime_pool`, then runner default. |
| `steps[].knowledge_context` | Step-level knowledge references merged with pipeline and task context. |

Common task directives:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `tasks[].name` | Required unique task name within the step. |
| `tasks[].goal` | LLM-backed task goal. Mutually exclusive with `script`. |
| `tasks[].script` | Direct shell task. Mutually exclusive with `goal`. |
| `tasks[].depends_on` | Dependencies inside the same step only. |
| `tasks[].ignore_failure` | Treats this task failure as ignored. |
| `tasks[].llm_output_sharing` | Task-specific output-history sharing override. |
| `tasks[].llm_profile` | Most-specific LLM model-client override for this task. |
| `tasks[].mcp_profiles` | MCP profiles for this LLM goal task. Invalid on script tasks. |
| `tasks[].variables` | Task-local environment overrides. Applied after step variables. |
| `tasks[].knowledge_context` | Most-specific knowledge references for this task. |

Approval directives:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `steps[].approval.type` | Required approval kind. Letters, numbers, underscores, dots, and hyphens are supported. |
| `steps[].approval.teams` | Required relative team paths assigned to approve or reject. |
| `steps[].approval.allow_self_approval` | Whether the original requester may approve the same checkpoint. |

## AI Directive Reference

AI controls are intentionally separate. Do not collapse these concepts in the
wiki:

| Directive or resource | Controls | Does not control |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `agent_profile` | Persona, role, and prompt instructions. | Provider, model, secrets, tools, credentials, permissions. |
| `llm_profile` | Provider, model, endpoint, credential reference, timeout, tokens, temperature, and provider-specific settings. | Persona, authorization, Knowledge Context, MCP server definitions. |
| `mcp_profiles` | Approved MCP servers and tool allowlists for LLM goals. | Arbitrary server URLs in pipeline YAML. |
| `knowledge_context` | Documents injected into LLM prompts and guardrail/policy behavior. | Runtime resource permission by itself. |
| AAA | Whether the original caller may use the selected resources. | Model selection or prompt wording. |

LLM profile resolution for conditions and LLM-backed tasks:

1. Task `llm_profile`
2. Step `llm_profile`
3. Pipeline `llm_profile`
4. Configured default profile

Final-output LLM profile resolution:

1. `output.items[].llm_profile`
2. `output.llm_profile`
3. Pipeline `llm_profile`
4. Configured default profile

Agent profile resolution:

1. Step `agent_profile`
2. Pipeline `agent_profile`
3. Configured default profile

MCP profile resolution is additive:

```text
 pipeline mcp_profiles
+ step mcp_profiles
+ task mcp_profiles
= deduplicated MCP profile set
```

Document the supported LLM providers in the wiki:

```text
gemini, lmstudio, openai, anthropic, groq, mistral, ollama, openrouter, azure-openai
```

`reasoning` and `thinking` are generic NopsAI options only for LM Studio. Other
providers reject generic values rather than silently ignoring stale config.

## Knowledge Context Reference

The wiki must explain both forms:

```yaml
knowledge_context:
  - kind: guardrail
    ref: security/repo-check
    required: true
  - kind: architecture
    path: .nopsai/docs/backend.md
```

Supported kinds:

```text
architecture, guardrail, policy, adr, guideline, runbook, reference, example
```

Rules:

- `kind` is required.
- Use exactly one of `ref` or `path`.
- Managed `ref` values use `team/document` format and require
  `knowledge_context.use`.
- Repo-local `path` values are loaded from the run repository at the run commit.
- `required: true` fails the run before execution when resolution or
  authorization fails.
- Guardrails and policies are blocking context. They apply to goals, commands,
  direct scripts, file writes, MCP calls, MCP arguments, and conditions.
- Resolved content is snapshotted on the run, so later document edits do not
  rewrite run history.

## Final Output Reference

Final outputs use:

```yaml
output:
  llm_profile: report-writer
  items:
    - name: Executive summary
      type: markdown
      when: success
      prompt: Summarize the successful run.
```

Supported `output.items[].type` values:

```text
markdown, pdf, excel, json, html, dashboard
```

Supported `output.items[].when` values:

```text
success, failure, always
```

Omitted or empty `when` is treated as `always`.

Run-list and run-detail documentation should explain that generated output
content stays on authorized detail/read paths, while list surfaces expose only
lightweight aggregate `final_output_status` metadata. The Pipeline Runs overview
table should describe successful output state as compact `success` text, while
details and related run panels may use richer labels and tooltips.

Dashboard output directives are valid only when `type: dashboard`:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `dashboard.ref` | Required `team/dashboard-slug` reference. |
| `dashboard.section` | Required dashboard section key. |
| `dashboard.entry_key` | Optional stable entry key for replace and series publications. Defaults to the output item name when omitted. |
| `dashboard.mode` | Optional publication mode: `replace`, `append`, `snapshot`, or `series`. Empty defaults to `replace`. |
| `dashboard.preset` | Optional prompt preset hint: `auto`, narrative-first `report`, primary `table`, current-state `status`, chronological `timeline`, side-by-side `comparison`, numbers-first `metrics`, or cohesive `mixed`. |
| `dashboard.ttl` | Optional stale-state duration such as `7d`. |

Final-output preview documentation should mention that ready output rows are
clickable preview toggles and that dashboard outputs can link directly to the
configured dashboard and section when `dashboard_target` metadata is available.

## Event Automation Reference

Git trigger manifests should document:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `provider` | Git provider metadata. Non-GitHub providers use managed Git Webhook Sources. |
| `team` | Owner team/application boundary for run ownership and inheritance. |
| `webhook_source` | Required source ID for GitLab, Bitbucket, Gitea, or generic providers. |
| `management` | Indicates NopsAI-managed trigger overrides. |
| `triggers[].on` | Event name; `all` matches any event. |
| `triggers[].branches` | Included branch globs. |
| `triggers[].skip_branches` | Excluded branch globs. |
| `triggers[].tags` | Tag globs for push tag events. |
| `triggers[].skip_repos` | Repository patterns that skip the rule. |
| `triggers[].include_paths` | Changed-file globs that must match when changed files are known. |
| `triggers[].exclude_paths` | Changed-file globs removed before include matching. |
| `triggers[].pipelines` | Pipeline identifiers started by a match. |
| `triggers[].scope` | Runtime scope for the run. |

Path matching intentionally fails open when changed-file metadata is unknown.
Missing provider data should not silently skip CI.
Forwarded GitHub App events from git-bot to `POST /v1/git/events` are internal
service calls and require a git-bot service token; this endpoint should not be
documented as public webhook ingress.

Schedules should document:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `schedule_kind` | `cron` or `once`. |
| `cron_expression` | Required for cron schedules; `cron` is accepted as an API alias. |
| `run_at` | One-time timestamp; enabled one-time schedules must be in the future. |
| `timezone` | IANA timezone, default `UTC`. |
| `pipeline` | Stored pipeline identifier. |
| `scope` | Runtime scope. |
| `run_team_path` | Pipeline Runs team path and notification lineage. `root` means unassigned root. |
| `variables` | Schedule-supplied run variable overrides. |

External triggers should document:

| Directive | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `allowed_callers` | Explicit `user`, `service_account`, or `auth_team` callers. |
| `variable_mapping` | Maps `event_type`, `payload.<path>`, `variables.<name>`, direct payload paths, or `literal:<value>` into run variables. |
| `payload_schema` | Object-schema guard with required fields and basic property type checks. |
| `rate_limit.per_minute` | Per-trigger invocation limit over the previous minute. |
| `idempotency_key` | Optional invoke-request retry key scoped by trigger and caller. |

## Current Product Boundaries

NopsAI is a self-hosted automation control plane for YAML-defined workflows
executed by Docker or Kubernetes runners.

Implemented workflow building blocks include:

- deterministic shell scripts
- LLM-backed goals
- reusable steps
- child pipelines
- human approvals
- scoped variables and encrypted secrets
- governed Knowledge Context
- LLM Profiles, Agent Profiles, and MCP Profiles
- manual, scheduled, Git, external-trigger, child-pipeline, and rerun entry
  points
- final Markdown, JSON, HTML, PDF, and Excel deliverables

The control plane includes the API, UI, AAA, dispatcher, git-bot, PostgreSQL,
Gotenberg, and the restricted Docker socket proxy. The execution plane includes
Docker runners, Kubernetes runners, per-run agents, step containers or pods, and
shared run workspaces.

## Supported Deployment Models

| Deployment | Use case | Runtime | Database |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Docker Compose | Development, demos, evaluation, and small single-host installs | Docker runner | Included PostgreSQL 15 |
| Release bundle | Version-pinned single-host deployment | Docker runner | Deployment-managed PostgreSQL |
| Helm/Kubernetes | Cluster-based production deployment | Kubernetes runner | External PostgreSQL |
| Hybrid runners | Central control plane with remote Docker or Kubernetes runners | Docker and Kubernetes runners | Control-plane PostgreSQL |

The Helm chart deploys API, AAA, dispatcher, git-bot, UI, Gotenberg, and the
Kubernetes runner. PostgreSQL and bootstrap secrets are intentionally external.

